Z||000007||operator64||04-25-2001||10:57 AM||||   I would have been more than happy for him to finish the inspection, but after i showed him the fuse block swung down his reply to me was " you need to fix it yourself and bring it back another time i'm not responsible to fix your car just so it will pass inspection." Indeed i did fix it myself and went down the street to finish what i started out to do. As for all the oddities in many cars most mechanics i'm associated with would have found a bit of humor in this situation but not him.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JSocha||04-25-2001||11:18 AM||||Really, this should go in the "Oil change" thread, but it also fits nicely here.<P>Dropped my GT at the service center first thing this morning.  New guy on board there says he has worked on Fiero's before at another station he had worked for cause I was wondering if he needed to know how to open anything.<P>Sitting here at work, receptionist buzzes me and passes me a call while I was helping our Draftsman with a computer question.<P>Its the newbie at our service station...one apparently with past experience on Fieros.<P>First question he asks has VW Bug written all over it for my response.<P>"Where's the motor at?"<P>Me:  "<B>WHAT!</B>  I just parked it there less then two hours ago and you mean to tell me somebody has already jacked it and ripped off my motor!?!?!?!"<P>&lt;silence&gt;<P>Me:  "Well, &lt;sigh&gt; don't worry about it...I think my insurance covers theft...And by the way, there's a spare motor in the trunk you can work on to get me by."<P>Him:  "Ahhhh F***!  Sorry.  Will be done at noon."<P>Ignorance is bliss!  And in Rugby, it shows that there are definately a lot of happy people!<P>As I stated, our draftsman was standing by my desk when this happened and he had to leave as he was laughing to hard that it brought tears to his eyes.  <P>Our new boss came by about the same time to say his standard "Good Morning" to everyone and was wondering what was going on that was so funny and I explained it to him what just happened and now he is laughing.||||reg||1
Z||000009||thomas_l||04-25-2001||11:21 AM||||&lt;Devils advocate mode&gt; firstfiero makes a valid point. In my past life being the tech (10 years) I had to face my share of know-it-alls that I was always dying to ask "If you're such an expert why did you bring it to me?"&lt;/Devils advocate mode&gt;<P>On the other hand, with a specialty or collectible car, it's not uncommon for the owner to be up to date on all the trivial stuff (like the flip-open fuse panel!). You learn something new everyday - don't be too surprised where you learn it from. FirstFiero: Take a chill pill and just grin real big when you hand the "teacher" your bill  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. My all-time favorite was a guy that brought me a '85 Mustang GT (5.0L carb) that he had already worked on - all new ignition parts, etc. - and it still ran like crap. He too had all the answers... except that one. He was a bit red in the face when he got the bill: $50 diagnostic time and a 25c piece of 3/8 vacuum tube that had a hole in it the size of a pencil. I grinned all week over that one!||||reg||6
Z||000010||firstfiero||04-25-2001||12:59 PM||||      first off i'm not in a bad mood damn it!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I just find it idiotic when people expect you to be perfect. That being said you didn't tell us in your original post that the guy acted like an a$$hole after you pointed it out.After what he said I would have also gladly taken my business elsewhere also. I'm completly understanding when someone makes a mistake..I loose that when afterwords they act like a jerk about it and/or don't seem to give a damn..<P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif"> <BR>Todd <BR>  86se||||reg||14
